  <div class="note note-warn">
    <h3>What diet can and cannot do</h3>
    <p>
      If your hair fall is caused by a deficiency — most often iron, sometimes vitamin D or
      protein — correcting it makes a real difference. If it is male or female pattern hair loss,
      diet supports treatment but will not stop it on its own. Which one you have is worth
      establishing before you rearrange your meals.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What matters, and where to get it</h2>
  
  <h3>Iron</h3>
  <p>
    The deficiency most often behind diffuse hair fall, particularly in women with heavy
    periods and in vegetarians. Plant iron is absorbed poorly on its own, so pair it with
    vitamin C in the same meal.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Rajma, chana, lobia, whole moong, masoor dal</li>
    <li>Palak, methi, bathua, sarson ka saag</li>
    <li>Ragi, bajra, jaggery, sesame seeds, dates</li>
    <li>Chicken, mutton liver, eggs, fish</li>
    <li><strong>Pair with:</strong> lemon on dal, tomato in sabzi, amla, orange or guava after the meal</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid with meals:</strong> tea and coffee, which reduce iron absorption — leave a gap of about an hour</li>
  </ul>
  
  <h3>Protein</h3>
  <p>
    Hair is largely protein. Indian vegetarian diets are often lower in it than people assume.
    Aim for a protein source at every meal rather than only at dinner.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Dal, rajma, chana, soya, paneer, tofu</li>
    <li>Curd, milk, buttermilk</li>
    <li>Eggs, chicken, fish</li>
    <li>Peanuts, almonds, walnuts, roasted chana as snacks</li>
    <li><strong>Combine</strong> dal with rice or roti in the same meal to complete the amino acid profile</li>
  </ul>
  
  <h3>Vitamin D</h3>
  <p>
    Widely deficient in India, including in people who are outdoors regularly. Food alone
    rarely corrects a real deficiency, so this is one to test rather than guess.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Around 15–20 minutes of sunlight on arms and face, mid-morning</li>
    <li>Fortified milk, eggs, fish such as rohu, surmai and sardines</li>
    <li>Mushrooms</li>
  </ul>
  
  <h3>Zinc, biotin and the rest</h3>
  <p>
    Zinc deficiency does cause hair fall, and is worth correcting where present. Biotin
    deficiency is genuinely rare, and biotin supplements do very little for people who are not
    deficient — despite being the main ingredient in most hair supplements sold in India. They
    also interfere with thyroid blood tests, which causes real confusion.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Pumpkin seeds, sesame, cashews, chana</li>
    <li>Curd, milk, eggs</li>
    <li>Chicken, seafood</li>
  </ul>
  
  <h2>A day, put together</h2>
  
  <table>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:26%">Early morning</th>
        <td>Soaked almonds and 2 dates, or a glass of water with amla</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:26%">Breakfast</th>
        <td>Besan chilla or moong dal chilla with curd · or 2 eggs with roti · or poha with peanuts, plus a fruit</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:26%">Mid-morning</th>
        <td>Buttermilk, or a guava or orange</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:26%">Lunch</th>
        <td>2 roti · dal or rajma · a green sabzi such as palak or methi · curd · salad with lemon</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:26%">Evening</th>
        <td>Roasted chana or a handful of peanuts and pumpkin seeds. Tea an hour away from meals</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" style="width:26%">Dinner</th>
        <td>Roti or rice · paneer, soya, chicken or fish · a vegetable · light and early where possible</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  
  <h3>Fasting days</h3>
  <p>
    On vrat days, keep protein in: curd, paneer, singhare ka atta with milk, and fruit with
    nuts. A day of only sabudana and potato is worth avoiding if hair fall is a concern.
  </p>
  
  <h2>What to be sceptical about</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hair gummies and multivitamin supplements.</strong> Mostly biotin, mostly unnecessary, and they disturb thyroid testing.</li>
    <li><strong>Detox and juice routines.</strong> No evidence for hair, and often low in protein.</li>
    <li><strong>Cutting out entire food groups.</strong> Restriction itself causes shedding — crash dieting is one of the commonest triggers seen in clinic.</li>
  </ul>

